Inferno/riscv64 native kernel, SUPERVISOR MODE under OpenSBI (QEMU -M virt)

This is the S-mode sibling of os/inferno/virt.  The virt board is
entered by QEMU with -bios none in machine mode at 0x80000000 and
drives the CLINT and M-mode CSRs directly.  This board is entered by
OpenSBI (QEMU's default -bios) in supervisor mode at 0x80200000, the
way real RISC-V hardware boots: firmware in M-mode, the kernel in
S-mode.  Everything portable is shared; only the mode-specific pieces
differ - and they are copies here, not #ifdefs, per the tree's rule.

What differs from virt:
  - l.s uses the S-mode CSRs (sstatus/sie/stvec/sepc/scause/stval) and
    returns from a trap with SRET, not MRET.  The interrupt-enable bit
    positions differ: sstatus.SIE is bit 1, sie.STIE bit 5, sie.SEIE
    bit 9.
  - The timer is armed through the SBI legacy set_timer ecall
    (sbisettimer in l.s), not a CLINT mtimecmp write - OpenSBI
    PMP-protects the CLINT from S-mode (see its Region02 print, S/U:
    none).  The monotonic tick is the time CSR (rdtime in l.s), for the
    same reason.  clock.c and stubs.c call these where virt touched
    ClintMtime/ClintMtimecmp.
  - trap.c reads the supervisor interrupt causes (timer 5, external 9)
    and drives PLIC context 1 (hart0 S-mode), where virt uses context 0
    (hart0 M-mode).
  - The link address is 0x80200000, above OpenSBI's own 0x80000000.

Build (from macOS host with KenC tools on PATH):

	export PATH=$ROOT/MacOSX/arm64/bin:$PATH ROOT=$ROOT
	cd os/inferno/virts
	rm -f ivirts		# force relink after lib changes
	mk ivirts

Boot (OpenSBI is the default -bios, so no -bios flag):

	qemu-system-riscv64 -M virt -m 512 -nographic -kernel ivirts

Add a disk to reach the device tests and shell:

	-drive file=blk.img,if=none,format=raw,id=blk0 \
	-device virtio-blk-device,drive=blk0

Init: os/inferno/init/virtinit.b rooted as /osinit.dis, same as virt.
Verified: boots clean through the full init battery (SNPRINT-ABI-OK
through ROOT-CMDS-OK) to the shell prompt, zero faults, 4/4.
